as I waited for the preview build of  ukulele to load i busied myself checking  Twitter and replying to emails there was  some music playing from my childhood I  couldn't quite remember I racked my  brain was it from Donkey Kong crash  bandicoot maybe banjo kazooie when I  looked up I realized I've been listening  to the song that greets you on ukuleles  Start screen and as I spent the next 90  minutes playing through a guarded demo  of the first two worlds I was hit with  wave after wave of nostalgia it's no  secret why this game taps into those  classic early 3d platformer memories  either made by platonic Games a company  made up of several key rare devs from  the n64 era ukulele is billed as a  spiritual successor to their most  cherished work from the past in ukulele  you play as the kind and sensible  chameleons UK and wisecracking bat laily  like banjo kazooie each member of the  duo has unique abilities you could can  eat honey or cannonballs and absorb  their properties allowing the team to  stick to slippery surfaces or withstand  powerful gusts of wind leili can use  sonar to reveal hidden platforms or flap  her wings to carry the pair across large  gaps just like jam jars in banjo tooie  abilities are unlocked through the  merchant character trouser a snake in a  safari hat that's squeezed into a very  tight but very sensible pair of shorts  he's not a charity either you need to  spend some of the 200 quills you can  collect from each of the games five  worlds to unlock any of the abilities  you need trouser quickly emerged as my  favorite character as he's prone to  answer his mid 80s cell phone mid  transaction or give the jaw advice  complete with superfluous esas because  he's a snake you're going to need all  the advice and abilities you can get  because ukuleles worlds are enormous  each can also be expanded with pages  another of the game's suite of  collectibles reminiscent of games like  Mario 64 or donkey kong 64 each world is  an open arena just begging to be  explored a forgiving checkpoint system  means an ill time jump or misjudgment of  distance doesn't see the last 10 minutes  of traversal going to waste exploration  is always rewarded to whether it be with  a new collectable a hidden cart racing  mini game or an encounter with one of  the many supporting characters there's  the scientist with octopus arms dr. paws  who can transform you Guren laily into a  twisted sentient flower in order to  pollinate other flowers Nimbo the cloud  needs help to make it rain because  they've lost their confidence and Clara  a skeletal explorer needs to be rescued  from a pot before she's tonight's dinner  every interaction animation and line of  dialogue is dripping in the same  sarcastic absurd humor rare was famous  for I kept catching sight of my stupid  ear-to-ear grin whenever Leigh Leigh  would crack wise with a meta joke about  Kickstarter money or modern games  reliance on tutorial levels these jokes  are delivered in classic rare fashion by  a scrolling dialog boxes accompanied by  varying squeaks and squawks it sound as  though their pitch shifted from the same  sample I'll cute at first it can quickly  get tiresome but thankfully you can  speed up dialogue with the press of a  button cutscenes are also delivered in  this way but the fantastic animation and  visual gags kept my attention throughout  speaking of you'll want to pay very  close attention as you play one of the  first boss battles you'll encounter is a  simple quiz against dr. quack one of the  game's main villains while similar to  grunty's furnace fun from banjo kazooie  or any of the quizzes for that matter  the new quiz has been expanded and now  adds in questions that are unique to  your playthrough for example I was asked  which ability i acquired first how many  total quills I'd collected and in a  classic nada banjo I had to identify a  location from a screenshot each time a  new question appeared I turn to other  people in the room and we excitedly  conferred about which we thought was the  correct answer ukulele is a game that  feels equal parts familiar and new it  captures the spirit and feel of the  games that defined rare in the n64 era  and brings them into the modern video  game landscape be sure to check out our  gameplay videos of ukuleles first two  worlds and subscribe for all of our  coverage on ukulele and video games in  general I'm Dan and I'm going to go and  play a bunch of banjo kazooie to tide me  over until April 11  here  [Music]
 

